{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father. Raymond III was a prominent figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a key role in the politics of the region.\n\n*Early Life and Reign*\nRaymond III was born around 1140 in Tripoli. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the sister of King Fulk of Jerusalem and Queen Melisende of Jerusalem. Raymond III became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after the death of his father. He was only around 12 years old at the time and his mother acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III's early reign was marked by conflicts with the Byzantine Empire and the Fatimid Caliphate. He also had to deal with the threat of the Crusader state of Antioch which was ruled by Prince Bohemond III. Raymond III was able to maintain good relations with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch and was able to secure the support of the Templars and the Hospitallers.\n\n*Capture and Imprisonment*\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din during the Battle of Harim. He was imprisoned in Aleppo for several years. During his captivity Raymond III was able to maintain his influence in the region and was able to secure the release of several other Christian prisoners.\n\n*Release and Later Life*\nRaymond III was released from prison in 1173 after a ransom was paid by the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos. He returned to Tripoli and resumed his role as Count. Raymond III continued to play a key role in the politics of the region and was a strong supporter of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He died in 1187 during the Siege of Acre.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.004263356793671846}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}